column1:
46:12|The nations have heard of your disgrace;
your cry fills the earth.
Warrior stumbles over warrior;
both fall together.|Keeps the linked falls and international hearing.

48:18|Come down from your glory;
sit in thirst, daughter dwelling in Dibon.
Moab's destroyer has come against you;
he has ruined your fortifications.|Follows the read imperative and keeps the unusual thirst image.

49:23|Concerning Damascus.
“Hamath and Arpad are shamed;
they have heard bad news and melt away.
Anxiety stirs the sea;
it cannot be still.|Keeps the pinned sea wording rather than silently emending it to hearts.

50:26|Come against her from every side.
Open her granaries;
pile her up like heaps of grain.
Devote her to destruction;
leave her no remnant.|Retains the harvest/storage images and absolute destruction command.

51:17|Everyone is senseless, without knowledge;
every metalworker is ashamed of the carved image.
The cast image is a lie;
there is no breath in them.|Keeps the craft criticism, singular/plural shift, and absence of breath.

51:30|Babylon's warriors have stopped fighting.
They stay in their strongholds;
their strength has failed;
they have become women.
Her homes are set on fire;
her gate bars are broken.|Retains the ancient gendered taunt, explained in notes, alongside concrete military collapse.

51:56|A destroyer has come against her, against Babylon.
Her warriors are captured;
their bows are broken.
For the **LORD** is a God of recompense;
he will surely repay.|Keeps repeated Babylon and emphatic repayment.
